# POSITIVE CONTROL for the specificity WAIVER itself.
#
# Identical in shape to MS7 — the same catcher string, matching zero baseline tests — but
# with NO `negative-control:` header. The gate must still SPEC FAIL it and exit 2.
#
# Why this file exists at all: a waiver that exempted EVERY entry produces exactly the same
# clean output as one that exempts only what it should. The three controls in the parent
# directory show the waived half (three SPEC WAIVED lines, then three verdicts, exit 1).
# This shows the other half, and the two together are what make the waiver evidence rather
# than an assertion.
#
#     MUTATION_DIR=mutations/negative-controls/waiver-control scripts/mutation-check.sh
#     # must exit 2 with: SPEC FAIL  M99: expect-test matches 0 baseline tests
#
# It lives in a SUBDIRECTORY because `registry()` globs `$MUT_DIR/MS*.mut` and
# `$MUT_DIR/M[0-9]*.mut` non-recursively: filed beside the three controls it would be
# picked up by their run and make it exit 2 at the specificity stage instead of exit 1
# with three verdicts, destroying the very behaviour that run exists to demonstrate.
id: M99
title: POSITIVE CONTROL - an undeclared entry whose catcher matches nothing
expect: caught
expect-test: a test that does not exist anywhere in this suite
file: src/lib/supabase/client.ts
count: 1
@@FIND
 * The ONE browser Supabase client, and the narrow `Database` type it is typed
@@REPLACE
 * The ONE browser Supabase client (positive control edit), and the narrow `Database` type it is typed
@@END
